{#- Home. Built from `content/` alone; every number below is measured, none is
    written into the template. The mockup's own copy quotes a journal count and
    a publication range, and both are wrong now — which is exactly why the
    chrome reads them from the archive instead. The figures themselves are not
    repeated here, so that a grep for them across the templates stays a useful
    check rather than one this comment defeats. -#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Urban Currents</title>
{{ head_extras }}
<style>
{{ css }}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main class="uc-page">
  {#- One list, from `site.nav_links` — the issue pages inject the same one
      (0Z, Z4). Two renderers is deliberate; two link lists was drift. -#}
  <nav class="uc-nav">
    {%- for link in nav %}
    <a{% if link.cls %} class="{{ link.cls }}"{% endif %} href="{{ link.href }}">{{ link.label }}</a>
    {%- endfor %}
    <span class="uc-nav__lang">EN</span>
  </nav>

  <header class="uc-mission">
    <h1 class="uc-mission__title">
      A daily scan of urban data science: what appeared, what it found, and why
      it matters.
    </h1>
    <p class="uc-mission__lede">
      {{ stats.arxiv_categories }} arXiv categories, {{ stats.journals }} journals —
      {% if stats.range_low == stats.range_high %}
      about {{ stats.range_low }} papers a day worth your time.
      {% else %}
      the {{ stats.range_low }}–{{ stats.range_high }} papers a day worth your time.
      {% endif %}
      When the signal is weak, we say so. Free, no ads, no engagement metrics.
    </p>
    {#- No subscription form. There is no domain, no sending infrastructure and
        no list, and a form that does nothing is a lie told in HTML. Phase 1
        fills this in when there is something behind it. -#}
  </header>

  {% if latest %}
  {#- Two columns, per mockup 5a: the lead on the left and a 210px stat rail on
      the right with a rule between them. It was one column, which is most of
      why the page did not look like the mockup. Flex rather than grid so 7b's
      mobile behaviour — the rail dropping below the lead as a row — is one
      `flex-direction` change. -#}
  <section class="uc-latest">
    <div class="uc-latest__head">
      <h2 class="uc-latest__title">Latest issue — {{ latest.date }}</h2>
      <a class="uc-latest__read" href="issues/{{ latest.date }}.html">Read →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="uc-latest__cols">
      <div class="uc-latest__lede">
        {#- The headline line whole. It is a full sentence and there is room for
            it; cutting it mid-clause in the template would be rewriting the
            pipeline's output at render time. -#}
        <p class="uc-latest__lead">{{ lead_line }}</p>
        {% if synthesis and synthesis.paragraph %}
        <p class="uc-latest__note">{{ synthesis.paragraph }}</p>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
      <dl class="uc-latest__stats">
        <div><dt>items</dt><dd>{{ latest.scan_meta.items_published }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>candidates scanned</dt><dd>{{ latest.scan_meta.candidates_scanned }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>with code / data</dt><dd class="uc-latest__accent">{{ latest_code }} / {{ latest_data }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>no open abstract</dt><dd class="uc-latest__quiet">{{ latest.scan_meta.unreadable_count }}</dd></div>
        {#- Three states, not two. A day with no shift and a day whose baseline
            was too thin to compare against are different facts, and printing
            `0` for the second is the mistake this project keeps auditing out
            of itself. -#}
        <div><dt>tag shift</dt><dd>{{ tag_shift }}</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  {% endif %}

  <section class="uc-archive">
    <div class="uc-archive__head">
      <h2 class="uc-archive__title">Archive — last {{ rows|length }} days</h2>
      <a class="uc-archive__all" href="archive.html">All issues →</a>
    </div>
    {#- Mockup 5a's 14-bar histogram. Height is proportional to what was
        published; the colour says which kind of day it was.

        **The colour is never the only carrier.** Every bar has a `title` and an
        `aria-label` naming the day, its count and its kind, so a reader who
        cannot see the accent — or is reading with a screen reader — gets the
        same three facts. That is 0j's verification 7, and it is why the caption
        under the strip spells out what grey means in words. -#}
    <div class="uc-spark" role="img"
         aria-label="Published items per day over the last {{ rows|length }} days.">
      {% for bar in spark %}
      <div class="uc-spark__slot{% if bar.recent %} uc-spark__slot--recent{% endif %}">
        <div class="uc-spark__bar uc-spark__bar--{{ bar.kind }}"
             style="height: {{ bar.height }}%"
             title="{{ bar.label }}"></div>
        <span class="uc-visually-hidden">{{ bar.label }}</span>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
    {#- Every state the strip can draw has a name here (0Z, Z8).
        `base.css.j2` states the rule the old caption only half kept: colour
        never carries the meaning on its own, so a state that is drawn is a
        state that is named. -#}
    <p class="uc-spark__caption">
      <span>{{ spark_from }}</span>
      <span class="uc-spark__legend">
        grey — a quiet day, nothing published ·
        hatched — not seen ·
        dashed — filled in later ·
        underlined — still filling in
      </span>
      <span>{{ spark_to }}</span>
    </p>
    {% with issue_href = "issues/" %}{% include "_archive_rows.html.j2" %}{% endwith %}
  </section>

  <section class="uc-columns">
    <div class="uc-column">
      <h3>Method</h3>
      <p>
        Two entry paths. A journal article enters because a journal we track
        published it; an arXiv preprint enters on a classifier's probability and
        only above a floor set from labelled examples. Neither path is asked to
        fill a quota.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="uc-column">
      <h3>Honesty</h3>
      <p>
        Bibliography, authors and links come from the sources, never from a
        model. Where a measurement was not possible we leave the line out rather
        than print a zero, and where it was possible and found nothing we say
        so.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="uc-column">
      <h3>Tags</h3>
      <p>
        Every tag carries a canonical identifier — OpenAlex, ORCID, ROR — so a
        method named twice is one thing, not two. Free strings are a test
        failure rather than a warning.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer class="uc-footer">
    <p class="uc-footer__ilp">Institute for Latent Publics · free, no ads, no metrics</p>
    <p class="uc-footer__build">urban-currents {{ pipeline_version }}</p>
    {# arXiv's terms require this wording exactly, including the disclaimer. #}
    <p class="uc-attribution">
      Thank you to arXiv for use of its open access interoperability. This
      service was not reviewed or approved by, nor does it necessarily express
      or reflect the policies or opinions of, arXiv.
    </p>
  </footer>
</main>
</body>
</html>
